// Scorching Ray. The beam is five parallel traces fired from ACS -- see "DnD Scorching Ray" -- so
// nothing here carries damage: these three are a ruler, a wall-finder and a trail.

// What the five damaging traces leave behind. It draws nothing and does nothing; the cast reads its
// position back by tid to work out what the ray struck, then deals the damage itself, once.
Actor Spell_ScorchRayPuff {
	// So the beam passes through PLAYERS rather than stopping on one. Same pair NoPuff carries, and
	// for the same reason: a trace fired from a helper does not know the caster, so without this it
	// would stop on the first teammate standing in front of the beam -- or on the caster.
	Species "Player"
	+THRUSPECIES
	+NOBLOCKMAP
	+NOGRAVITY
	+NOINTERACTION
	+DONTSPLASH
	+ALWAYSPUFF
	+PUFFONACTORS
	RenderStyle None
	Radius 1
	Height 1
	States {
		Spawn:
			TNT1 A 1
		Stop
	}
}

// The FAKE hitscan's puff, for the visual cutoff. THRUACTORS is the whole point: it ignores monsters
// and stops only on geometry, so where it lands is where the wall is -- which is where the trail has
// to end. Without it the beam would stop at the first enemy and leave the rest of its length unlit.
Actor Spell_ScorchRayMarker : Spell_ScorchRayPuff {
	RenderStyle Add
	Scale 0.75
	+THRUACTORS
	States {
		Spawn:
			WIBL JKLMN 2 Bright
		Stop
	}
}

// One bead of the beam. Clientside and non-interacting: the damage is settled in ACS long before any
// of these exist, so they only have to look right.
//
// It lives exactly ONE TIC, because the whole beam is respawned every tic to follow the player's aim.
// Anything longer and the beads left behind by a turn hang in the air as a stale arc -- the beam has
// to be redrawn, not trailed.
// Each frame lasts TWO tics, not one. The beam is respawned every tic to follow the player's aim, so
// one looks right -- but an actor created and destroyed inside a single tic can be removed by the
// thinker pass before it is ever drawn, and the whole beam was invisible while the script spawning it
// ran perfectly. Two is the shortest life that reliably renders, and the second tic fades, so a fast
// turn smears instead of leaving a hard stale arc.
Actor Spell_ScorchRayTrail {
	+NOINTERACTION
	+CLIENTSIDEONLY
	+DONTSPLASH
	RenderStyle Add
	Alpha 0.85
	Radius 4
	Height 8
	States {
		Spawn:
			TNT1 A 0 NoDelay A_Jump(256, "1", "2", "3", "4")
		1:
			WIBL A 1 Bright
			WIBL AA 1 Bright A_FadeOut(0.33)
		Stop
		2:
			WIBL B 1 Bright
			WIBL BB 1 Bright A_FadeOut(0.33)
		Stop
		3:
			WIBL C 1 Bright
			WIBL CC 1 Bright A_FadeOut(0.33)
		Stop
		4:
			WIBL D 1 Bright
			WIBL DD 1 Bright A_FadeOut(0.33)
		Stop
	}
}
